# How to Write a Good Prompt
### DBS Finance AI Hub · Prompting Guide

---

## Why Prompting Matters

Claude is a powerful AI assistant, but it is not a mind reader. The quality of its output is directly proportional to the quality of your input. A vague prompt produces a vague answer. A well-structured prompt produces a precise, usable output — the first time.

This guide will show you exactly how to write prompts that work, with real examples drawn from Finance workflows.

---

## The CROFT Framework

Use **CROFT** as your mental checklist before sending any prompt.

| Letter | Stands For | What to Include |
|--------|------------|-----------------|
| **C** | **Context** | Who you are, what you're working on, background the AI needs |
| **R** | **Role** | What role you want the AI to play |
| **O** | **Output Format** | How you want the response structured (table, bullet, paragraph, JSON) |
| **F** | **Focus / Constraints** | Boundaries, rules, what to include or exclude |
| **T** | **Task** | The specific thing you want done |

You don't need all five elements in every prompt — but the more you include, the better the result.

---

## Common Prompting Mistakes to Avoid

**Too vague:**
> "Summarise this."

**Too assumptive** (assumes AI knows your context):
> "What's the variance this month?"

**No output format specified:**
> "Analyse the P&L."

**Asking too many things at once:**
> "Summarise, translate, reformat, and flag anomalies in this report."

